<div id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze the sensitivity of OLMo models to post-training quantization (PTQ) on both in-domain and out-of-domain data.
Figure <a href="#A1.F22" title="Figure 22 ‣ A.3.3 Post Training Quantization Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> shows that, across all geometric metrics, models trained with weight decay <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> perform consistently worse under PTQ for the smaller OLMo-4M and OLMo-8M models.
Conversely, the least impacted models across sizes tend to exhibit lower effective rank <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math>, lower cosine similarity <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math>, and higher isotropy <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast, representation-level metrics, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math>, do not display consistent trends: <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math> remains largely invariant, while <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math> spans a wide range among well-performing models.
The regression results in Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.3.3 Post Training Quantization Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> follow the expected pattern, with high raw and Chinchilla-adjusted Spearman correlations but weak partial correlations and negligible predictive <math id="A1.SS3.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Together, these findings indicate that most variance in PTQ robustness is not explained by the considered geometric metrics, suggesting the influence of additional underlying mechanisms.
Results for out-of-domain PTQ in Figure <a href="#A1.F23" title="Figure 23 ‣ A.3.3 Post Training Quantization Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> and Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ A.3.3 Post Training Quantization Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> exhibit similar trends, further reinforcing our conclusions about the limited explanatory power of geometric metrics for PTQ behavior.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.3.4 </span>Fine-Tuning Evaluation.</h4>

