<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 6</span>: </span><span id="A1.T6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fine-tuning Loss (Starcoder Python) correlation analysis summary.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The fine-tuning scatter plots for StarCoder-Python and OpenWebMath are shown in Figures <a href="#A1.F24" title="Figure 24 ‣ A.3.4 Fine-Tuning Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F25" title="Figure 25 ‣ A.3.4 Fine-Tuning Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>, respectively.
For smaller models (4M-16M), the unembedding metrics <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> exhibit substantial variance, while representation metrics <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math> are tightly clustered.
In both cases, these behaviors indicate limited predictive value for fine-tuning performance.
For the larger models (28M-75M), lower effective rank <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> and higher cosine similarity <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> are more frequently associated with better performance, though this trend is neither strong nor consistent.
Isotropy <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}(\mathbf{W})</annotation></semantics></math> shows a weak linear relationship with fine-tuning loss for larger models, but this pattern does not hold for smaller models.
Supporting regression analyses in Tables <a href="#A1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ A.3.4 Fine-Tuning Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#A1.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ A.3.4 Fine-Tuning Evaluation. ‣ A.3 Extended Discussion: The Impact of Model Geometry on Downstream Performance ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Disentangling Geometry, Performance, and Training in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> confirm these observations, with most metrics exhibiting weak partial Spearman correlations.
Although <math id="A1.SS3.SSS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐇</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}(\mathbf{H})</annotation></semantics></math> surprisingly, shows a relatively strong association for StarCoder-Python, this effect does not generalize to OpenWebMath.
Overall, none of the considered geometric metrics consistently predict fine-tuning performance across both datasets.</p>
</div>
